course: biology
lesson: 394
title: How nature documentaries are made: hides, camera traps, drones and years of waiting
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: You watch a snow leopard creep across a cliff for three thrilling minutes. Behind those minutes there may be months of a crew sitting in freezing tents, hidden cameras on rocks and hundreds of hours of film that nobody ever sees.
explain: Wild animals stop behaving naturally when they see or smell people, so film-makers hide. They sit in hides made of branches, leave camera traps that film by themselves, fly quiet drones overhead and even build robot animals with cameras inside.
explain: Special cameras show what our eyes cannot: high-speed cameras slow a hummingbird's wings, time-lapse speeds up a flower opening, and heat cameras see warm animals glowing in the dark.
explain: Most crews also follow a rule: do not interfere. They film what happens, even when it is sad, because nature has to take its course. People still argue about the rare times crews have helped.
fact: Wildlife film-makers sit in hides so animals carry on as if nobody is there, because animals that see or smell people often stop what they are doing or run away; some crews waited in hides for weeks to film one short scene.
fact: Camera traps are boxes that take pictures or film when an animal passes in front of them; they can wait for months in places people rarely go, and they have filmed rare animals like snow leopards.
fact: A normal film shows about 25 pictures every second, while high-speed cameras film thousands a second, so one fast second can last for minutes when played back.
fact: In time-lapse the camera takes one picture every few minutes over days and then shows them quickly, making the slow movements of plants and fungi visible.
fact: Drones are small flying cameras that film animals from above without a helicopter; they are quieter but must be flown carefully so animals, especially nesting birds, are not scared.
fact: Scientists use drones to count whales and even to collect whale breath from the spray of their blow.
fact: Infrared and heat cameras film at night by catching light or warmth our eyes cannot see; warm animals glow bright on heat cameras, while cold ground looks dark.
fact: Some divers use special breathing machines that let out no noisy bubbles, because bubbles and noise scare many fish; some deep scenes are filmed from small submarines.
fact: A single scene of a rare animal can take years to film, because weather, luck and the animal must all be right; an hour-long programme may be made from hundreds of hours of film, and most is never shown.
fact: Some film-makers built animal-shaped robots with cameras, such as a robot penguin, because animals often ignore a robot that looks and moves a bit like them.
fact: Scientists also fix small cameras to animals to see the world from the animal's point of view.
fact: Most nature film crews follow a rule not to change what happens; once in a while crews have helped, for example by digging a ramp for trapped penguins, and people still argue whether that was right.
exercise: Why do film-makers sit in hides? | So animals carry on as if nobody is there | To keep the cameras dry when it rains hard | To scare the animals towards the camera so they run past it | To keep the animals locked inside the hide
exercise: How long have some camera people waited in a hide for one scene? | Weeks | A few seconds | Exactly one hour | Never more than a morning
exercise: What does a camera trap do? | Takes pictures or film when an animal passes in front of it | Catches the animal in a cage so the crew can film it later at home | Calls the animal over with recorded sounds | Follows the animal through the forest
exercise: Which rare animal have camera traps filmed? | Snow leopards | Dodos | Mammoths | Unicorns
exercise: About how many pictures a second does a normal film show? | About 25 | About 2 | About 1000 | About a million
exercise: What do high-speed cameras let us see? | Fast things like a hummingbird's wings shown slowly | Animals that are invisible to our eyes even in daylight | Things that are very far away on the Moon | Sounds turned into colours
exercise: What does time-lapse show? | Slow movements of plants and fungi sped up | Fast animals like cheetahs slowed right down so we can see their legs | Animals moving about in the dark | Pictures taken only from space
exercise: Why must a drone pilot keep far from nesting birds? | The drone can scare the birds off their nests | Birds always attack drones and break their propellers | Drones cannot fly anywhere near trees or bushes | Birds steal the drone's camera
exercise: What have scientists collected with drones flying over whales? | Whale breath from the spray of their blow | Whale teeth that had fallen out into the water | Whale songs written down on paper | Whale eggs floating on the surface of the sea
exercise: How do heat cameras film animals at night? | They catch warmth our eyes cannot see | They shine a bright torch on the animals | They listen to the animals' hearts | They wait until the Moon is full
exercise: Why do some underwater film-makers use breathing machines with no bubbles? | Bubbles and noise scare many fish | Bubbles crack the glass in front of the camera | Fish eat the bubbles and get ill | Bubbles make the water around the diver too warm
exercise: About how much film might be shot for one hour-long programme? | Hundreds of hours | Exactly one hour | Ten minutes | A whole year with no breaks
exercise: Why did film-makers build a robot penguin with a camera inside? | Penguins often ignore a robot that looks and moves like them | To teach the young penguins how to swim and dive under the ice | To replace penguins that had died | To scare away the hungry seals
exercise: Why do scientists fix small cameras to animals? | To see the world from the animal's point of view | To keep the animals warm on cold nights | To make the animals famous on television | To stop the animals from moving about
exercise: What is the usual rule for nature film crews? | Do not change what happens, let nature take its course | Always rescue every animal that gets into any kind of trouble | Feed the animals so they stay near | Move the animals to better places
exercise: What did one crew do that people still argue about? | Dug a ramp for trapped penguins | Painted brighter stripes on a zebra | Taught a lion to roar on time | Built a whole new nest for an eagle
test: «why's the show only 1 hour if they filmed for 3 years, lazy or what» What is the real reason? | Most film is waiting, and only the best moments make it in | They lost the rest of the film on the way home from the jungle | Animals only move for one hour a year | The crew slept for most of the three years
test: What would happen if a film crew walked noisily up to a family of foxes in the open? | The foxes would probably stop what they were doing or run away | The foxes would stop and pose for the camera, like people do for a photo | The foxes would act the same as always | The foxes would follow the crew home
test: Count them: a camera films 25 pictures a second. How many pictures in 4 seconds? | 100 | 29 | 50 | 125
test: In a cartoon, a cameraman stands in the open in a bright shirt and wild animals dance in front of him. What do real crews do? | Hide or use remote cameras so animals behave naturally | Wear bright clothes, because the colours attract the animals closer | Ask the animals politely to stand still | Film only animals that live in cages
test: «saw a vid where a mushroom grew in 5 seconds, faked innit» What is the most likely explanation? | It is time-lapse: pictures taken over days shown quickly | Mushrooms really can grow from nothing in five seconds after rain | It was just a cartoon mushroom | The mushroom was pulled up with a string
test: On a heat camera at night, a fox and a cold rock are in the picture. What will you see? | The fox glowing bright and the rock dark | The rock glowing and the fox dark | Both exactly the same grey | Nothing, heat cameras see only in daytime
test: You want to film a hedgehog in your garden at night without disturbing it. What would work best? | A camera trap with a night camera | Standing right next to it with a torch | Shouting so that it comes out of the hedge | Picking it up to film it close
test: «they just stood there filming while the baby elephant was stuck, cold or what» Why do crews usually not help? | Their rule is not to change what happens, though people argue about it | Crews are not allowed to touch anything except their cameras and tripods | Elephants always get out alone | They were too far away to see it
test: A robot penguin waddles among real penguins and films them. Why does it work? | Real penguins often ignore a robot that looks and moves a bit like them | Penguins cannot see at all, so they never notice anything new around them | The robot speaks penguin language | Penguins love all machines
test: Why might a diver with a bubbling mask film fewer fish than a diver with a no-bubble machine? | Bubbles and noise scare many fish away | Bubbles make the water dark | Fish are drawn only to quiet divers' torches | Bubbles hide the camera lens
test: Count them: a crew films 300 hours and shows 1 hour. How many hours are never shown? | 299 | 300 | 301 | 30
test: Which animals would glow brightest on a heat camera on a cold night? | Warm-blooded mammals and birds | Cold-blooded frogs and snakes | Stones and logs that soaked up the sun | Dead leaves lying on the ground
exam: Identify the principal reason for which wildlife film-makers conceal themselves within hides. | Animals which perceive human beings frequently cease their natural behaviour or take flight | Hides are required principally in order to protect the cameras and other equipment from rainfall | Concealment permits the film-makers to capture the animals | Animals are attracted to the scent of human beings
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the technique of time-lapse photography? | Pictures are taken at intervals over a prolonged period and subsequently displayed in rapid succession | Thousands of pictures are taken each second and are subsequently displayed at a greatly reduced speed | Pictures are taken solely in the absence of light | A single picture is displayed for a prolonged period
exam: Select the response consistent with the established practice of most crews engaged in the filming of wild animals. | Intervention in natural events is ordinarily avoided, although exceptional interventions remain the subject of debate | Every animal found in difficulty is invariably rescued by the crew, irrespective of the circumstances or of the risk | Animals are regularly fed in order to retain them before the camera | Animals are relocated to more suitable habitats prior to filming
exam: It is given that a normal film displays approximately twenty-five pictures per second. Ascertain the number of pictures displayed in a period of ten seconds. | Two hundred and fifty | Thirty-five | Twenty-five | Two thousand five hundred
